Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.50,0:00:06.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This paper asks a fundamental question in learning theory: how well does plain logistic
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.09,0:00:10.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}regression solve the problem of agnostically learning halfspaces?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.26,0:00:12.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}A halfspace is a linear classifier,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.51,0:00:17.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and in the agnostic setting an adversary corrupts an OPT fraction of the labels,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.65,0:00:21.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so the best any linear classifier can do is a zero-one error of OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.02,0:00:27.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Prior work left a wide gap: a lower bound saying no convex surrogate can beat order OPT,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:27.67,0:00:33.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and an upper bound showing logistic regression achieves only order square-root of OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.20,0:00:34.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The authors close this gap.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.93,0:00:40.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}They construct a well-behaved distribution on which the global minimizer of the logistic
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.59,0:00:43.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}risk provably suffers square-root OPT error,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.41,0:00:45.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}matching the known upper bound exactly.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.92,0:00:51.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}They then show that adding a natural radial-Lipschitzness assumption lets logistic regression
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.89,0:00:54.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}reach near-optimal order-OPT error.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.14,0:00:57.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Finally, they give a strikingly simple two-phase algorithm,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:57.93,0:01:01.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}logistic regression followed by a perceptron step,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.14,0:01:05.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}that attains near-optimal error for any well-behaved distribution,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.38,0:01:09.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}replacing the many rounds of optimization that prior methods required.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.18,0:01:14.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper studies the agnostic learning of homogeneous halfspaces,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.87,0:01:19.48,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}one of the most fundamental problems in machine learning.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:19.56,0:01:24.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}We are given samples from an unknown distribution over feature-label pairs,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.25,0:01:28.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and we want a linear classifier whose zero-one error is close to OPT,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.94,0:01:33.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the best error achievable by any homogeneous halfspace.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:33.63,0:01:38.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Equivalently,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:38.32,0:01:42.93,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}an adversary is allowed to flip an OPT fraction of the labels of an otherwise perfectly linearly separable dataset.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.01,0:01:47.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Logistic regression is the natural, ubiquitous heuristic here,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.70,0:01:52.31,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}yet its theoretical guarantees for this problem were poorly understood.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.39,0:01:57.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Prior results left a frustrating gap: a lower bound saying no convex surrogate loss can beat order OPT error,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.08,0:02:01.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}against an upper bound showing logistic regression reaches only order square-root of OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.77,0:02:06.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The central question is which of these bounds reflects the truth for logistic regression.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:06.76,0:02:11.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Logistic regression is arguably the most widely deployed classification algorithm,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.95,0:02:16.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so understanding its statistical guarantees is not merely academic.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.19,0:02:20.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The worry is that logistic regression can behave terribly in the worst case;
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.00,0:02:26.36,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}earlier work showed its risk minimizer can be wrong on nearly a one-minus-OPT fraction
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.44,0:02:29.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}of examples on an adversarially built distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.67,0:02:34.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}To rule out such pathologies, the community focuses on well-behaved distributions,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.86,0:02:39.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}such as isotropic log-concave ones, where much stronger guarantees are possible.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:39.92,0:02:45.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But even under these assumptions the precise error rate of logistic regression was unknown,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:45.68,0:02:49.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}sitting somewhere between order OPT and order square-root OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:49.61,0:02:54.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Pinning down this rate tells us whether logistic regression is a near-optimal agnostic
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.05,0:02:59.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}learner or whether it fundamentally leaves accuracy on the table,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:59.16,0:03:02.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and if so, what minimal fix recovers optimality.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:02.50,0:03:06.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper makes three contributions that together resolve the gap.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.63,0:03:10.31,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First, it constructs an explicit two-dimensional, isotropic,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.39,0:03:15.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}well-behaved distribution on which the global minimizer of the logistic risk provably
Dialogue: 0,0:03:15.71,0:03:18.07,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}attains square-root OPT zero-one error,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:18.15,0:03:23.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}matching the known upper bound and proving that logistic regression alone cannot do better.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.84,0:03:26.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second, it identifies a clean sufficient condition,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:27.03,0:03:29.21,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}radial Lipschitzness of the density,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:29.29,0:03:33.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}under which logistic regression does reach the near-optimal order-OPT rate.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:33.98,0:03:35.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Third, and most usefully,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.55,0:03:39.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it introduces a simple two-phase algorithm: run logistic regression,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:39.80,0:03:45.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}then run a perceptron-style hinge-loss step from that warm start on a restricted domain.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.31,0:03:51.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This attains near-optimal error for every well-behaved distribution without the radial-Lipschitz assumption,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.07,0:03:55.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}at the cost of just one additional convex optimization,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.51,0:04:02.44,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and it is far simpler than prior algorithms that required solving a logarithmic number of minimization problems.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.82,0:04:06.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The technical core rests on two convex surrogate losses.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.36,0:04:10.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The logistic loss is the log of one plus e to the minus margin,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.34,0:04:14.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the hinge loss is the positive part of the negative margin;
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.31,0:04:17.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}both are minimized as empirical averages over the samples.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.98,0:04:19.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For the lower bound,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:19.24,0:04:23.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the authors hand-design a distribution Q on the plane with four parts,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.66,0:04:27.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a construction valid when OPT is at most one over sixteen,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:27.32,0:04:32.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}engineered so that the true optimal halfspace is only wrong on a small noisy region,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.63,0:04:39.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}yet the logistic risk minimizer is pulled into a direction that misclassifies a square-root OPT fraction.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.26,0:04:41.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For the upper bound and the algorithm,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.66,0:04:47.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the key object is the angle between the learned direction and the ground-truth direction.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.28,0:04:54.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Phase one runs projected gradient descent on the logistic risk inside a ball of radius one over square-root epsilon,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:54.61,0:05:00.71,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which under well-behaved-ness returns a direction within angle order square-root OPT of the truth.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.79,0:05:05.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Phase two then runs projected stochastic gradient descent on the hinge loss,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.59,0:05:08.61,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which is exactly the classical perceptron update,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:08.69,0:05:12.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}starting from that warm start and confined to a restricted domain.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:12.86,0:05:18.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}This second convex step sharpens the angle and drives the zero-one error down to near order OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:19.22,0:05:21.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Because this is a learning-theory paper,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.65,0:05:24.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}there are no benchmark datasets or empirical tables;
Dialogue: 0,0:05:24.80,0:05:30.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the "data" are distribution classes and the results are theorems with explicit constants.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:30.21,0:05:36.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The positive results hold for the standard family of well-behaved distributions used in prior work,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:36.22,0:05:43.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}meaning the marginal on features is isotropic and satisfies soft-margin and sub-exponential regularity conditions.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.13,0:05:50.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The negative result is carried by a concrete counterexample: an explicit distribution Q on the two-dimensional plane,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.24,0:05:54.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}built from four parts and illustrated in the paper's single figure,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.30,0:05:57.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}that is fully well-behaved yet defeats logistic regression.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.88,0:06:00.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The upper bounds are stated in two regimes,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.49,0:06:04.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}one for bounded feature distributions where the norm is at most B,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.50,0:06:07.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and one for sub-exponential feature distributions,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:07.53,0:06:12.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so the guarantees span the settings that matter for isotropic log-concave data.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:12.63,0:06:15.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The headline finding is a matching pair of bounds.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.93,0:06:18.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}On the constructed well-behaved distribution,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:18.90,0:06:26.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the global minimizer of the population logistic risk has zero-one error at least square-root OPT divided by sixty pi,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:26.63,0:06:32.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which asymptotically matches the best known upper bound and therefore proves that square-root
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.77,0:06:36.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}OPT is the true rate for logistic regression alone.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:36.14,0:06:40.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}That is the sense in which the paper closes the long-standing gap.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:40.50,0:06:43.86,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Then, by adding radial Lipschitzness of the density,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:43.94,0:06:48.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the same logistic minimizer jumps to near-optimal error of order OPT,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:48.49,0:06:53.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}up to a constant Cκ that is bounded whenever the density is Lipschitz.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:53.12,0:06:55.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And without any such assumption,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.23,0:07:00.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus epsilon error for bounded distributions
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.98,0:07:04.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and order OPT times log one over OPT for sub-exponential ones,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:05.07,0:07:06.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}in expectation over the run.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.22,0:07:11.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Although this is a theory paper without experimental ablations,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:11.10,0:07:15.71,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the analysis is structured to expose which assumptions buy which guarantees,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:15.79,0:07:17.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}playing the role an ablation would.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:17.95,0:07:23.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The single upper-bound argument is versatile enough to recover the earlier square-root
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.25,0:07:27.24,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}OPT rate for general well-behaved distributions as a special case,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:27.32,0:07:32.30,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and crucially it also goes through for the hinge loss, not just the logistic loss;
Dialogue: 0,0:07:32.38,0:07:36.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}this dual applicability is what enables the perceptron second phase.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:36.57,0:07:40.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The theorem cleanly separates the bounded and sub-exponential regimes,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:40.89,0:07:46.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}showing that the only price of moving from bounded to heavier sub-exponential tails is
Dialogue: 0,0:07:46.19,0:07:48.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}an extra logarithmic factor in one over OPT.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:48.90,0:07:53.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Likewise, the constant Cκ, an integral of the radial-Lipschitzness modulus,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:53.53,0:07:57.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}isolates exactly how much the density's radial smoothness matters,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:57.60,0:08:00.54,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}vanishing when the density is radially symmetric.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:00.92,0:08:05.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}A few numbers capture the impact.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.48,0:08:09.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The lower bound gives a zero-one error of at least square-root OPT over sixty pi for the logistic minimizer,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:10.03,0:08:14.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}valid whenever OPT is at most one one-hundredth.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:14.59,0:08:19.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Under radial Lipschitzness, logistic regression reaches near-optimal order-OPT error.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:19.14,0:08:23.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The two-phase algorithm achieves order OPT plus epsilon error for bounded distributions
Dialogue: 0,0:08:23.70,0:08:28.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and order OPT times log one over OPT for sub-exponential ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:28.25,0:08:32.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}It does so with a sample complexity of order d over epsilon squared,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.81,0:08:37.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a quadratic improvement over the d over epsilon to the fourth required by prior nonconvex methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:37.37,0:08:41.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}And structurally,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:41.92,0:08:46.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}whereas earlier algorithms that hit order OPT error had to solve a logarithmic number
Dialogue: 0,0:08:46.48,0:08:50.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}of minimization problems while guessing OPT by binary search,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:51.03,0:08:55.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}this method needs only two convex steps.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:55.89,0:08:59.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The lasting message is twofold.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:00.04,0:09:04.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:04.19,0:09:08.27,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}logistic regression by itself cannot beat square-root OPT error for agnostically learning halfspaces,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.35,0:09:12.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the paper proves this rate is exactly tight by constructing a distribution that forces it.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:12.50,0:09:16.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:16.65,0:09:20.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the fix is remarkably cheap: appending one perceptron-style convex minimization,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:20.80,0:09:24.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}warm-started from the logistic solution and confined to a bounded domain,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:24.96,0:09:29.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}provably boosts the guarantee to near-optimal order-OPT error for every well-behaved distribution.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:29.11,0:09:33.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Along the way the paper also identifies radial Lipschitzness of the feature density as
Dialogue: 0,0:09:33.26,0:09:37.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the natural condition under which logistic regression alone already succeeds.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:37.41,0:09:41.49,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}In short, a passerby can remember that logistic regression is half the answer,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:41.57,0:09:45.64,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and a single extra convex step completes it,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:45.72,0:09:49.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}more simply and with better sample complexity than any prior method.
